## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
According to the third law of thermodynamics, perfect crystals at absolute zero have an entropy of zero. What does this law imply about the entropy of imperfect crystals at absolute zero?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The third law of thermodynamics states that the entropy of a perfect crystal at absolute zero is zero. This is because at absolute zero, all isatomic motion is absent, and the system is in its lowest energy state, which is in equilibrium. Therefore, the disorder or randomness (entropy) is minimized.

In the case of **inperiodic crystals (imotropic crystals)** or **imperiodic structures (discrystalline structures)** at absolute zero, the third law implies that their entropy is not zero. At absolute zero, the entropy of an impure crystal is $\boxed{\text{not zero}}$.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

