3. $f(x-1) > f(3-2x)$.

Since $f(x)$ is increasing on $[0, \infty]$, the inequality $f(x-1) > f(3-2x)$ implies that $x-1 > 3-2x$ for $x-1$ and $3-2x$ in $[0, \infty]$.

Solving $x-1 > 3-2x$:
$x - 1 > 3 - 2x$
$3x > 4$
$x > \frac{4}{3}$

Now, we check the conditions $x-1 \geq 0$ and $3-2x \geq 0$:
$x - 1 \geq 0 \implies x \geq 1$
 $$ 3 - 2x \geq 0 \implies 3 \geq 2x \implies x \leq \frac{3}{2} $$

