An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \), follow these steps:

1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf1} \):**
   The closed-loop gain is given by:
   \[
   A_{vf1} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{100}{1.1} \approx 90.0 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

2. **Calculate the new open-loop gain \( A_{vf2} \):**
   The gain decreases by 6 dB, so the new output gain is:
   \[
   A_{v2} = 100 - 6 = 94 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf2} \):**
   Using the same feedback ratio \( m_v = 0.1 \), the new closed-loop gain is:
   \[
   A_{vf2} = \frac{A_{v2}}{1 + m_v} = \frac{94}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{94}{1.1} \approx 85.1 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

4. **Calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain:**
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf2} - A_{vf1}}{A_{vf1}} \right) \times 100\% = \left( \frac{85.1 - 90.0}{90.0} \right) \times 100\% \approx -1.1 \text{ dB dB}
   \]

   The negative sign indicates a decrease in the closed-loop gain.",relevance,"# Task Description
