where \(\beta = \frac{v}{c}\) is the velocity of the electron. 

The final answer is:
\[
Q^2 = \frac{1}{2} (p_i^2 + p_f^2 - 2 p_i \cdot p_f)
\]
This is derived from the definition of the four-momentum transfer \( q = P_i - P_f \). The square of the four-momentum transfer is:
\[
Q^2 = q^2 = (P_i - P_f)^2 = P_i^2 - 2 P_i \cdot P_f + P_f^2
\]
Substituting the expressions for \( P_i \) and \( P_f \):
\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_i}{c} \right)^2 - 2 \left( \frac{E_i}{c} \right) \left( \frac{E_f}{c} \right) \cos \theta + \left( \frac{E_f}{c} \right)^2
\]
Since the electron mass is negligible, \( E = p c \), so:
\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{p_i c}{c} \right)^2 - 2 \left( \frac{p_i c}{c} \right) \left( \frac{p_f c}{c} \right) \cos \theta + \left( \frac{p_f c}{c} \right)^2
\]
\[
Q^2 = p_i^2 + p_f^2 - 2 p_i p_f \cos \theta
\]
This is equivalent to the dot product of the momentum vectors:
\[
Q^2 = p_i^2 + p_f^2 - 2 p_i \cdot p_f
