\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) \times (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]  
Now, calculate the acceleration:  
\[
a = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) Using the kinematic equation for constant acceleration:  
\[
v = u + at
\]  
Given \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), \(u = 0 \, \text{m/s}\) (since the proton starts from rest), and \(a = 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\). Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 - 0}{4.79 \times 10^{10}} = 7.33 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}
\]  
