","To solve this problem, we need to consider the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation occurs when an object is moving at a high speed relative to an observer. The time experienced by the object (in this case, the clocks on the spaceship) will be shorter than the time experienced by the observer (in this case, the clock on Earth).

The formula for time dilation is:

t' = t * sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

where t' is the time experienced by the moving object (the spaceship clocks),
t is the time experienced by the observer on Earth,
v is the relative velocity of the moving object (0.6c),
c is the speed of light.

First, we need to find the time experienced on Earth (t) when the clock in the cockpit shows 3 years (t'). We can rearrange the formula to solve for t:

t = t' / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

t = 3 years / sqrt(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
t = 3 years / sqrt(1 - 0.36)
t = 3 years / sqrt(0.64)
t = 3 years / 0.8
t = 3.75 years

So, the clock on Earth shows 3.75 years when the clock in the cockpit shows 3 years.

