The Gibbs free energy change for the reaction is given by:

\[
\Delta G = \Delta G_f(\text{products}) - \Delta G_f(\text{reactants})
\]

Given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation for water (\( \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}) \)) is \(-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}\), we can calculate the Gibbs free energy change for the reaction:

\[
\Delta G = 2 \times \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}) - (2 \times \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2) + \Delta G_f(\text{O}_2))
\]

