\[
I(\lambda) = \frac{h c^2}{2\pi \lambda^4} e^{-\frac{hc}{\lambda kT}}
\]  
where:  
- \( I(\lambda) \) is the intensity of thermal radiation at wavelength \( \lambda \).  
- \( h \) is the Planck constant (\( 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s} \)).  
- \( c \) is the speed of light (\( 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \)).  
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation.  
- \( T \) is the absolute temperature of the black body (in Kelvin).  
- \( k \) is the Boltzmann constant (\( 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{J/K} \)).  

This equation describes the spectral distribution of thermal radiation, showing how the intensity of the radiation depends on the wavelength at a given temperature.  

