To compute the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system, we use the formula:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
V_x & V_y & V_z
\end{vmatrix}.
\]
Since \(\mathbf{V}\) is a function of \(x\) and \(y\), we can write \(\mathbf{V} = V_x(x, y) \mathbf{i} + V_y(x, y) \mathbf{j} + 0 \mathbf{k}\). Thus, \(V_z = 0\). Substituting this into the curl formula:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \0 \\
V_x(x, y) & V_y(x, y) & 0
\end{vvmatrix}.
\]
Expanding the determinant along the third component:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y} V_x - 0\right) \mathbf{i} - \left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x} V_x - 0\right) \mathbf{j} + 0 \mathbf{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x}\right) \mathbf{k}.
\]

Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is \(\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x}\right) \mathbf{k}}\).
