","The mass and composition of a star play a crucial role in determining the final fate of its gravitational collapse. According to general relativity, the gravitational force exerted by a massive object, such as a star, can be so strong that it causes the star to collapse under its own gravity. The exact outcome of this collapse depends on the star's mass and the properties of its matter.

The mass of a star is the primary factor that determine the final state of the collapsed star. The composition of the star also affects the collapse process, as it affects the density and pressure balance of the star.

The general fate of a star's collapse can be summarized as follows:

1. Massive stars (greater than 10 times of the mass of the Sun) will eventually undergo a supernova explosion, and after the explosion, the core of the star will continue to collapse. If the mass of the core is greater than the Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff (TOV) limit (around 2 to 3 solar masses), the core will collapse under its own gravity to form a black hole. If the mass of the core is below the TOV limit, the core will collapse to form a neutron star.

2. Low-mass stars (less than 8 times of the mass of the Sun) will eventually exhaust their nuclear fuel and exhaust own outer layers into a nebula, and the core of the star will collapse to form a white dwarf.

3. Stellar collapse of stars with masses between  8 and 10 times of the mass of the Sun would be still uncertain, as they could potentially form a black hole or a neutron star.

