","To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction, we first need to write the balanced chemical equation:

\[
2 \text{H}_2(g) + \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{H}_2\text{O}(g)
\]

The Gibbs free energy change for the reaction is given by:

\[
\Delta G = \Delta G_f(\text{products}) - \Delta G_f(\text{reactants})
\]

Given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation for water (\( \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}) \)) is \(-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}\), we can calculate the Gibbs free energy change for the reaction:

\[
\Delta G = 2 \times \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}) - (2 \times \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2) + \Delta G_f(\text{O}_2))
\]

For standard conditions (298 K and 1 bar), the Gibbs free energy of formation for H₂ and O₂ is zero:

\[
\Delta G_f(\text{H}_2) = 0 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]
\[
\Delta G_f(\text{O}_2 = 0 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]

Thus, the Gibbs free energy change is:

\[
\Delta G = 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) - (2 \times 0 \text{ kJ/mol} + 0 \text{ kJ/mol}) = -474.2 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]

However, the question asks for the change in Gibbs free energy for 2 moles of hydrogen gas reacting with 1 mole of oxygen gas. Since the standard Gibbs free energy of formation is given per mole of product, the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction is:

\[
\Delta G = 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) = -474.2 \text{ kJ}
\]

